course: geography
lesson: 54
title: Glaciers and ice ages
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A glacier is a river of ice. Snow piles up year after year, gets squashed into ice, and the ice becomes so heavy that it slowly flows downhill, often only a few metres a day.
explain: As it moves it scrapes the land like giant sandpaper with rocks frozen into its base. It turns a narrow V-shaped river valley into a wide U-shaped valley with a flat floor and steep sides; when the sea floods one, we get a fjord, like the ones in Norway.
explain: For the last 2.6 million years Earth has swung between cold glacial periods and warmer interglacials. About 20,000 years ago ice covered much of Britain, so much water was locked up that the sea was about 120 metres lower and you could walk to Europe across Doggerland.
explain: Imagine a boulder as big as a car sitting alone in a field, made of rock found nowhere nearby: ice carried it there. Today almost all mountain glaciers are shrinking fast because of human-caused warming, and hundreds of millions of people depend on rivers they feed.
fact: A glacier is a huge mass of ice that slowly flows downhill under its own weight.
fact: Today two great ice sheets cover Greenland and Antarctica and hold about 99% of the world's glacier ice.
fact: Glaciers carve wide valleys with flat floors and steep sides, called U-shaped valleys.
fact: Fjords are long, deep sea inlets made when the sea flooded valleys carved by glaciers.
fact: During the last 2.6 million years the Earth has swung between cold glacial periods and warm interglacials.
fact: About 20,000 years ago ice sheets covered much of Britain, Scandinavia and northern North America.
fact: In the ice age, sea level was about 120 metres lower, so Britain was joined to Europe by a land called Doggerland.
fact: Glaciers dropped huge boulders, called erratics, far from the rocks they came from.
fact: From about 1300 to 1850 Europe had a cooler period called the Little Ice Age, with glaciers advancing in the Alps and the Thames in London sometimes freezing hard enough for fairs on the ice.
fact: The Little Ice Age was much milder than a real ice age, and scientists still discuss its exact dates and causes.
fact: Today almost all mountain glaciers are shrinking fast because of human-caused global warming.
fact: Mountain glaciers feed rivers that hundreds of millions of people depend on, especially in Asia.
exercise: What is a glacier? | A huge mass of ice that slowly flows downhill | A frozen lake high in the hills that never moves | A cloud made of ice crystals | A snowy mountain top with no ice under it at all
exercise: What makes a glacier move? | Its own great weight | The wind pushing it along | Fish swimming under it | The Moon pulling it upwards
exercise: Where are the world's two great ice sheets today? | Greenland and Antarctica | Iceland and northern Norway | Siberia and northern Canada | The Alps and the Himalayas
exercise: About how much of the world's glacier ice do the two ice sheets hold? | About 99% | About 50%, half of it | About 10% of it | About 1%, hardly any
exercise: What shape of valley does a glacier carve? | A U-shape with a flat floor and steep sides | A narrow V-shape like a mountain stream cuts | A round bowl with no sides | A zigzag shape
exercise: What is a fjord? | A long, deep sea inlet in a valley carved by a glacier | A wide river delta made of mud where a river meets the sea | A waterfall over hard rock | A small pond in a meadow
exercise: For about how long has the Earth swung between glacial and interglacial periods? | About 2.6 million years | About 2,600 years, since the Romans | About 26 years | About 26 billion years, longer than time
exercise: About when did ice sheets last cover much of Britain? | About 20,000 years ago | About 200 years ago | About 2 million years ago only | About 2,000 years ago
exercise: How much lower was the sea in the last ice age? | About 120 metres | About 1 metre, hardly at all | About 12 km, miles down | It was higher, not lower
exercise: What was Doggerland? | Land that joined Britain to Europe when the sea was low | A frozen island near the North Pole where hunters lived | A mountain range in Scotland | A city built on a glacier
exercise: What is an erratic? | A big boulder a glacier dropped far from where it came from | A deep crack in the ice of a glacier that walkers fall into | A lake at the end of a glacier | A rock that floats on water
exercise: What was the Little Ice Age? | A cooler time in Europe from about 1300 to 1850 | A real ice age with ice over all of Europe | A glacier the size of a house | A winter in 2010
exercise: What is happening to almost all mountain glaciers today? | They are shrinking fast because of human-caused warming | They are growing fast because the mountains are getting colder | They have stopped changing | They are moving uphill
exercise: Why do mountain glaciers matter to hundreds of millions of people in Asia? | They feed the rivers people depend on | They are the only source of salt in Asia | They keep the volcanoes from erupting | They make the sea warmer
exercise: Which of these did NOT happen in the last ice age? | Ice covered the whole Earth, even the tropics | Ice covered much of Scandinavia | Sea level was much lower | Britain was joined to Europe
test: In a cartoon a glacier zooms down the mountain like a car. What should be corrected? | Glaciers flow very slowly, often a few metres a day | Glaciers do not move at all, they just sit and melt | Glaciers move only uphill | Glaciers slide fast only at night, when it is cold
test: You hike in Norway along a long, narrow arm of the sea with cliffs on both sides. What carved it? | A glacier, before the sea flooded the valley | A wide river winding slowly through a hot desert | Waves cutting a cave that later fell in | An earthquake opening a crack
test: A farmer finds a huge granite boulder in a field where all the rocks below are chalk. How did it most likely get there? | Ice carried it there and dropped it | It grew there slowly from a tiny seed | Wind blew it there from far away | People made it in a factory and lost it
test: What would happen to sea level if lots of ice on land melted? | It would rise | It would fall | It would stay exactly the same | The sea would freeze
test: An early hunter 12,000 years ago wants to walk from England to the Netherlands. Could he? | Yes, across Doggerland while the sea was low | No, the sea was even deeper then than it is now | Only by boat across a wide ocean | No, people did not exist then
test: "glaciers r just snow that never melted, they dont even move" — Tom. Fix it. | Glaciers are snow squashed into ice that flows downhill | Tom is right, glaciers never move | Glaciers are frozen sea water that floats | Glaciers are made of white rock
test: Paintings of London show people having fairs on the frozen Thames in the 1600s. Which cold time fits? | The Little Ice Age | The last great ice age | The Jurassic period | The Cambrian explosion
test: Why does a U-shaped valley tell a geologist that a glacier was once there? | Glaciers carve wide, flat-floored valleys; rivers carve V-shapes | U-shaped valleys are made only by wind | Rivers always make U-shapes, so a U shows an old river was there | U-shaped valleys appear after earthquakes
test: What would happen to some big rivers of Asia in the dry season if their mountain glaciers vanished? | They could carry much less water | They would carry far more water for ever | They would turn salty | They would flow backwards
test: Which is true about ice ages? | Earth has swung between cold and warm periods for 2.6 million years | There has only ever been one ice age, and it ended when the dinosaurs died | Ice ages happened only in Africa | The last ice age ended last century
test: An old photo from 1900 shows a glacier filling a valley; today it is a small patch of ice far up. Why? | Most mountain glaciers are shrinking fast with warming | Glaciers always grow in summer | Someone dug the glacier out | The photo shows a different planet
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a glacier? | A mass of ice that proceeds downhill under its own weight | A stationary body of frozen fresh water | A formation of snow suspended in the atmosphere | A deposit of crystalline rock resembling ice
exam: Identify the principal reason for which sea level stood approximately 120 metres lower during the most recent glacial period. | Vast quantities of water were retained as ice upon the land | The oceans evaporated entirely into space | The sea floor was elevated by volcanoes | The Moon ceased to exert any pull
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning erratics. | They are boulders transported and deposited by glaciers far from their origin | They are fissures within glacial ice | They are lakes situated within volcanic craters formerly occupied by glaciers | They are deposits formed by desert winds
exam: Ascertain the present condition of the majority of the world's mountain glaciers. | They are diminishing rapidly in consequence of human-caused warming | They are expanding rapidly in all regions | They have remained unaltered since 1850 | They have entirely disappeared
